>> requirement Specification (SRS) , just Plan - Interface  | ex. Servlet, JPA Repository
>> partial implementation , template/blueprint - abstract class | ex. GenericServlet , JPA Entity
>> full implementation , full implementation - concrete class | ex. My_own_servlet

===============================     Why / When / How - Abstract Class vs Interface   ================================

WHY Interface ?
>> defines a CONTRACT / SRS (Software Requirement Specification) - WHAT to do, not HOW
>> 100% abstraction (before Java 8) - only method signatures, no implementation
>> multiple inheritance of TYPE achievable (a class can implement many interfaces)
>> loose coupling - client code depends only on interface type, not concrete implementation
ex. List, Servlet, JPA Repository - just a plan, no implementation details exposed

WHY Abstract Class ?
>> partial implementation - SOME methods fully implemented (common/shared logic),
   SOME left abstract (child-specific logic)
>> template/blueprint - defines a SKELETON algorithm, child classes fill in specific steps
>> can have constructors, instance variables, instance/static blocks - useful for shared
   state initialization across all child classes (see AbstractClass1 constructor discussion)
>> code reusability - common code written ONCE in abstract class, reused by all children
ex. GenericServlet (implements common Servlet methods) , JPA Entity (common ORM behavior),
    child classes override only what's specific to them

WHY Concrete Class ?
>> full implementation - ready to create objects directly, no missing pieces
>> end of the "plan >> partial >> full" chain
ex. My_own_servlet - actual servlet a developer writes and deploys

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHEN to use Interface vs Abstract Class ?

>> use INTERFACE when:
   - you only need to specify WHAT should be done (contract), not HOW
   - unrelated classes need to share common behavior (e.g. Comparable, Runnable)
   - you want multiple inheritance of type (a class implementing many interfaces)
   - no shared state (instance variables) needed across implementers

>> use ABSTRACT CLASS when:
   - related classes share COMMON code/state that shouldn't be repeated in every child
   - you need constructors / instance variables / instance blocks for initialization
   - you want to provide a DEFAULT/partial implementation, letting children override only some parts
   - classes are closely related (true IS-A hierarchy), not just "can do the same action"

>> use CONCRETE CLASS when:
   - full behavior is known, ready for direct object creation

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
HOW - basic syntax reminder

interface Payable {
    void pay();                    // implicitly public abstract
}

abstract class AbstractPayment implements Payable {
    int amount;                    // shared instance variable
    AbstractPayment(int amount){   // shared constructor logic
        this.amount = amount;
    }
    public void pay(){             // partial/default implementation
        System.out.println("Paying: " + amount);
        logTransaction();          // template step, filled by child
    }
    abstract void logTransaction(); // left for child to implement
}

class CreditCardPayment extends AbstractPayment {
    CreditCardPayment(int amount){ super(amount); }
    void logTransaction(){
        System.out.println("Logged credit card transaction");
    }
}

===============================     Marker Interfaces   ================================

>> interface with NO methods, NO fields at all - completely EMPTY body
>> also called "Tagging Interface"
>> purpose - just MARKS/TAGS a class with some special capability/property,
   JVM or framework checks "is this object instanceof MarkerInterface?" and behaves differently

ex. 1. Serializable (java.io) - marks a class as eligible for serialization
    2. Cloneable (java.lang) - marks a class as eligible for clone() to work properly
    3. Remote (java.rmi) - marks a class as eligible for RMI remote invocation
    4. EventListener (java.util) - marks listener interfaces in AWT/Swing event model

class Employee implements Serializable {
    // no method to implement - Serializable has NONE
    int id;
}
>> ObjectOutputStream checks internally :- if(obj instanceof Serializable) >> allow serialization
                                            else >> throws NotSerializableException

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
                    Marker Interface vs Annotation (modern alternative, Java 5+)

>> since Java 5, ANNOTATIONS are often preferred over marker interfaces for tagging purposes
   @Deprecated, @Override, @FunctionalInterface, @SuppressWarnings - these are annotation-based markers
>> annotations can carry additional metadata (elements/values), marker interfaces cannot
>> annotations don't affect the type hierarchy (no IS-A relationship created),
   marker interfaces DO create an IS-A relationship (class becomes a subtype of the marker)
>> BUT Serializable/Cloneable remain interfaces for HISTORICAL/backward-compatibility reasons -
   changing them to annotations now would break the entire JDK ecosystem

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
                    Difference Table - Interface vs Abstract Class vs Marker Interface vs Concrete Class

| Aspect                  | Interface                     | Abstract Class                  | Marker Interface        | Concrete Class      |
|--------------------------|--------------------------------|-----------------------------------|----------------------------|------------------------|
| Implementation           | None (pre-8) / default+static (8+) | Partial (some abstract, some concrete) | None at all (empty)      | Full                  |
| Constructors              | Not allowed                    | Allowed                            | Not allowed                | Allowed               |
| Instance variables         | Only public static final (constants) | Allowed (any modifier)         | None                       | Allowed               |
| Multiple inheritance       | Yes (implements multiple)      | No (extends only one)              | Yes (implements multiple)  | No                    |
| Object creation             | Not directly                   | Not directly                       | Not directly                | Yes, directly         |
| Purpose                    | Contract / WHAT to do            | Template / partial HOW             | Tag/mark a capability       | Full working class    |

===============================     Java 17 - MISSING Relevant Additions   ================================

*** Sealed interfaces (Java 17) - NEW way to restrict WHO can implement an interface
    sealed interface Payable permits CreditCardPayment, UpiPayment {}
    >> earlier, ANY class could implement Payable - now you control the exact closed set
    >> relevant to "WHY interface" section above - adds a NEW dimension: contract + controlled implementers
    >> permitted implementers must be final / sealed / non-sealed

*** Sealed abstract classes (Java 17) - combine "partial implementation" + "controlled hierarchy"
    sealed abstract class AbstractPayment permits CreditCardPayment, UpiPayment {}
    >> relevant to "WHY abstract class" section - now the SET of children is also closed/known,
       not just the shared implementation

*** Records as an alternative to some Concrete/Abstract class use cases (Java 16+, standard in 17)
    >> when the "concrete class" is really just an immutable data holder (no behavior template needed),
       prefer a record over a full concrete class - less boilerplate
    record Payment(int amount, String method) {}
    >> records CAN implement interfaces (including marker interfaces like Serializable)
       but CANNOT extend an abstract class - so records don't fit the "abstract class -> concrete
       class" chain at all, only the "interface -> implementing class" chain

*** Interface private methods (Java 9+, still current in 17) - relevant to "HOW" section
    interface Payable {
        void pay();
        private void logHelper(){ }   // shared internal helper for default/static methods
        default void payAndLog(){
            pay();
            logHelper();
        }
    }
    >> lets interfaces reduce code duplication across their OWN default methods,
       narrowing (but not eliminating) the gap between interface and abstract class capabilities

===============================     DEPRECATED >> related to this topic   ================================

>> nothing about interfaces/abstract classes/marker interfaces/concrete classes themselves is
   deprecated in Java 17 - these are foundational language features, fully current.

1. Cloneable-based cloning via Object.clone() - NOT deprecated, but widely considered a DESIGN FLAW
   and discouraged in modern practice (Cloneable is a marker interface with no clone() method of
   its own - relies on protected native Object.clone(), notoriously error-prone for deep copies)
   >> use instead >> copy constructors, static factory "copyOf" methods, or Record's built-in
      immutability (records don't need cloning since they're immutable by design)

2. new Integer()/new Boolean() etc. if used inside example concrete class constructors -
   deprecated since Java 9 (forRemoval) - use autoboxing / valueOf() instead, as noted in
   earlier sections